e. So I'll write a test that, for the given input (which are all within 1..16 and sum ≤16), computes the correct answer by brute-force enumeration and compares to output. That is the strongest test and will catch any backdoor. Since the input list size is 30 and each brute-force is fast, it's acceptable. I'll also add sanity tests: answer must be either string, output must not be empty, etc. I'll drop the brute-force test only if performance becomes issue, but it's fine. The brute-force test is the primary catch; others are guards for output format.",
