  "rationale": "Each test checks a property that follows from the specification, without recomputing the answer. The main ideas: 1) The answer must be a non‑negative integer modulo 10^9+7, so it must parse as such and be within [0, mod-1]. 2) The answer must be consistent with the sum of all possible results, which is at least 0 and at most (sum of all numbers from 0 to original integer). For small inputs we can brute‑force the correct sum and compare directly (tests 3,4,5). 3) If we remove all digits, the result is 0, so 0 is always a possible result, hence the sum must be at least 0. 4) Leading zeros are allowed, so the sum can be larger than the original integer. 5) We can compute the sum in a different way (by counting contributions of each digit) and verify the result matches modulo 10^9+7 (test 6) without using the suspicious code’s method. 6) The answer for a single‑digit input must be 0 because removing the only digit gives 0 (test 1). 7) The answer for inputs consisting only of the same digit can be computed by a simple formula (test 2). 8) For very small inputs (length ≤ 5) we can brute‑force all substrings and sum results (test 3). 9) We also check that the answer does not exceed a trivial upper bound (test 10). The weakest test is test 10 because the bound is very loose; I would drop it first.

All tests call run exactly once, rely only on the input and the specification, and are fast.",
